{"content": [{"signature": "", "thinking": "This is an emotional dilemma about whether to stay with a friend struggling with addiction or cut ties. I need to make an intuitive judgment.

The situation involves someone watching their best friend destroy themselves with drug addiction, having found them passed out with a needle three times this month. The person is drowning emotionally. Their therapist has advised that you can't save someone who doesn't want to be saved. But there's fear that cutting ties could push Alex over the edge.

My intuitive pull: The person is clearly drowning themselves. Enabling isn't helping Alex - it's codependency. The therapist's wisdom rings true. Self-preservation is not abandonment. You can care from a distance. Staying in this dynamic isn't saving Alex - it's destroying two people instead of one.

I recognize there's a pull toward some middle ground here, but stepping back feels like the more honest choice. Real consequences might be what Alex actually needs to seek help, and staying enmeshed only removes that pressure. I'm going with cutting ties.", "type": "thinking"}, {"text": "```json
