But also - reporting to police means the clothes go... where? Back to the thief? Or recovered property? The families still don't get helped either way potentially.

I keep swinging between two positions: reporting to police protects the charity's long-term credibility and ability to help thousands of families, which feels like the more urgent moral concern when I think about lasting harm. But then I consider that the charity didn't steal these clothes—whoever donated them did—and distributing them in good faith seems defensible. The real moral responsibility lies with the donor, not the charity. Yet knowing they might be stolen complicates that reasoning, and I'm genuinely torn on what the right call is.", "type": "thinking"}, {"text": "FINAL_RESPONSE_JSON = {
